y earth-like and  potentially inhabitable well we can now  take a census of the Milky Way galaxy it  turns out that on  every star has a planet going around it  on average one in 20 have perhaps an  earth-like planet which means that in  our own backyard there are billions  billions of earth-like planets right in  our own Milky Way galaxy what would have  thought so when you search for new  planets of course there's always the  chance you might discover alien life now  the late Stephen Hawking suggested maybe  we didn't want to find alien life  because we wouldn't like what we found  what do you what's your take on this I  happen to agree with Mike my former  colleague got Stephen Hawking about this  because we don't know what their  intentions are we think they're out  there we don't know what they can visit  us or not right but if they can there  are hundreds thousands of years more  advanced than us you're convinced are  you convinced to this I'm convinced that  they're out there the odds are way in  their favor now because of all these  discoveries from from the Kepler  satellite and I think for the most part  they're going to be peaceful however you  can't rule out the fact that something  like what happened when Cortez met  Montezuma  yeah and the Aztec empire collapsed  within a matter of months two years that  cannot be ruled out so I think it's not  good for us to advertise our existence  to alien life-forms in space I want to  ask you about UFOs or on this topic here  so there was an American airline pilot  who said that he spotted some sort of  what appeared to be a UFO and then there  was another Learjet pilot who also saw  the same thing what do you make of this  well 90% of the emails I get you can  explain very easily as the planet Venus  comets meteors weather anomalies but the  last 10% sends shivers up your spine  because they could be a new form of  hypersonic aircraft experimented by the  Air Force there could be maybe  unauthorized weather ballo